<!--
    kuna `rettriangle`: `returndup` gives each predecessor of a shared
    constant-return block its own return, which recovers a source's
    early-return guards.  At the join of a one-block `if` there is no guard to
    recover, and the split printed `if (c) celebrate(); return 0;` as
    `if (!c) return 0; celebrate(); return 0;`.

    Stage / sub-stage: P8 "goto-quality".  The P0 assertion
    `option rettriangle on|off` decides whether `returndup` declines a return
    block whose two predecessors are a two-way condition and a block entered
    only from that condition that runs into the return.

    The bytes are functions of decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/
    rettriangle.c built by gcc 11.4 at -O0 with -fstack-protector-strong
    (x86-64 SysV): finish (`if (ok) celebrate(); return 0;`), check_name (the
    same `if` behind a stack-protected buffer, whose stripped canary check
    leaves the shared return), guard_first (a source guard clause, which
    compiles to the same shape) and two_way (two conditions, no one-block
    `if` at the return, the control).

    Pass 1 runs with no option line, the shipped default (off), and
    reproduces the split.  Pass 2 (`option rettriangle on`) prints each
    one-block `if` with one return; two_way is the same in both.
-->
